1
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:12,275
When is a string just a string — and when does it become a security problem?

2
00:00:12,276 --> 00:00:25,023
To answer that, we need to see the boundary between memory regions
that may be physically adjacent, but are logically separate.

3
00:00:25,024 --> 00:00:38,410
This is Phase One of my C++ memory-safety deep dive: visualizing
what happens when input length and buffer capacity do not match.

4
00:00:44,310 --> 00:00:52,834
Before we look at memory regions, we need to
understand what is actually being stored.

5
00:00:52,835 --> 00:00:57,097
A byte is made up of eight bits.

6
00:00:57,098 --> 00:01:01,969
The same byte can be represented in several ways.

7
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:09,276
In binary, the letter A is written as 0100 0001.

8
00:01:09,277 --> 00:01:14,757
In hexadecimal, the same value is written as 0x41.

9
00:01:14,758 --> 00:01:20,238
As an ASCII character, it is simply the letter A.

10
00:01:20,239 --> 00:01:29,981
These are not three different values
They are three different ways of representing the same byte.

11
00:01:29,982 --> 00:01:42,770
Later, when we examine the memory dump, we will read the data in
all three forms: offsets, hexadecimal values, and ASCII characters.

12
00:01:49,010 --> 00:01:57,759
The C++ program creates one contiguous byte array with twenty bytes of storage.

13
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:02,470
That array is divided into three logical regions.

14
00:02:02,471 --> 00:02:07,854
The first eight bytes represent the logical buffer.

15
00:02:07,855 --> 00:02:12,566
The next four bytes represent a critical flag.

16
00:02:12,567 --> 00:02:17,277
The final eight bytes represent a second buffer.

17
00:02:17,278 --> 00:02:30,065
The important distinction is this: the bytes are physically
adjacent, but each region still has its own logical boundary.

18
00:02:30,066 --> 00:02:46,218
In other words, being next to another region does not automatically
mean that the first buffer owns it or is allowed to write into it.

19
00:02:46,219 --> 00:02:59,680
This is a deliberately constructed educational model
It is not a claim about the layout of a real compiler-generated stack frame.

20
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:11,035
Now we will start with an input that fits.

21
00:03:11,036 --> 00:03:16,472
The input is HELLO, which contains five bytes.

22
00:03:16,473 --> 00:03:28,705
The logical buffer has a capacity of eight bytes,
so all five input bytes remain inside the buffer.

23
00:03:28,706 --> 00:03:34,821
The adjacent flag and the second buffer are unchanged.

24
00:03:34,822 --> 00:03:38,219
This is the safe case.

25
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:54,530
Notice that input length and buffer capacity are different quantities
Here, the input is smaller than the capacity, so there is no boundary crossing.

26
00:04:04,610 --> 00:04:09,340
Now the input is much longer: ABCDEFGHIJKLMNO.

27
00:04:09,341 --> 00:04:18,801
This input contains fifteen bytes, while the logical buffer can hold only eight.

28
00:04:18,802 --> 00:04:26,234
The first eight bytes fit inside the buffer: A through H.

29
00:04:26,235 --> 00:04:29,613
Then we reach the boundary.

30
00:04:29,614 --> 00:04:39,750
In this deliberately constructed model, the remaining
bytes appear in the adjacent logical regions.

31
00:04:39,751 --> 00:04:52,590
I through L replace the bytes representing the critical flag,
and M, N, and O replace the beginning of the second buffer.

32
00:04:52,591 --> 00:05:03,403
This visualization shows why input length
and buffer capacity must be treated as separate quantities.

33
00:05:03,404 --> 00:05:15,567
In a real unsafe implementation, bytes that continue
past a buffer's boundary may overwrite adjacent data.

34
00:05:15,568 --> 00:05:23,001
That can corrupt program state and may create a security vulnerability.

35
00:05:23,002 --> 00:05:29,083
However, this specific Phase One program is still a safe model.

36
00:05:29,084 --> 00:05:34,490
It is not performing an actual out-of-bounds write.

37
00:05:45,070 --> 00:05:52,597
We can now read the same memory as a dump.

38
00:05:52,598 --> 00:06:10,163
The first column shows the offset. The second column shows the byte in hexadecimal
The third column shows its ASCII interpretation.

39
00:06:10,164 --> 00:06:21,874
For example, A is represented as 0x41, J as 0x4a, and O as 0x4f.

40
00:06:21,875 --> 00:06:46,132
The dump makes the boundary crossing visible. At offsets eight through eleven,
the flag bytes are no longer zero. They now contain the bytes for I, J, K, and L.

41
00:06:46,133 --> 00:06:58,680
This is the low-level view of the same event we just watched in the animation.

42
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:16,926
Let us be precise about what this demonstration does — and does not — show.

43
00:07:16,927 --> 00:07:23,532
Phase One does not perform an actual out-of-bounds write.

44
00:07:23,533 --> 00:07:38,945
The program copies at most the total size of the twenty-byte array
Every copied byte therefore remains inside that educational model.

45
00:07:38,946 --> 00:07:47,752
We are visualizing where bytes would land if they continued across logical regions.

46
00:07:47,753 --> 00:08:00,963
In real C++ code, an access outside the bounds of an array is undefined behavior
The result is not guaranteed.

47
00:08:00,964 --> 00:08:10,504
The program might appear to work, produce incorrect output, corrupt data, or crash.

48
00:08:10,505 --> 00:08:17,844
That is why this phase focuses on the mental model first.

49
00:08:17,845 --> 00:08:28,120
The next phase will isolate an intentionally vulnerable C-string operation
and examine it with AddressSanitizer.

50
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:46,593
Phase Two will show the vulnerable operation:
an unchecked C-string copy using strcpy,

51
00:08:46,594 --> 00:08:53,589
where the destination capacity is not provided to the function.

52
00:08:53,590 --> 00:09:04,957
We will then compile the isolated program with AddressSanitizer
and examine the diagnostic report.

53
00:09:04,958 --> 00:09:18,949
But this phase ends with the central lesson:
input length is not the same as buffer capacity.

54
00:09:18,950 --> 00:09:36,440
Once we can see that boundary clearly, we can understand both the vulnerability
and the defenses used to reduce its risk.

